from lib import *
C=9;S='maths'
CH='set-language'
lesson(C,S,CH,'sets-and-representation',"Sets, their representation and types","The school bag","Your school bag holds a set of books. Mathematics uses the word set for any well-defined collection of objects, and it is the language in which later ideas are written.",
["A set is a well-defined collection of distinct objects, called its elements; we write x belongs to A as x in A. @ set",
 "Roster form lists the elements: A = {1, 2, 3, 4}. Set-builder form gives a rule: A = {x : x is a natural number less than 5}. @ roster and set-builder",
 "The number of elements of a finite set A is its cardinal number, n(A). @ n(A)",
 "The empty set has no elements and is written as { } or the symbol phi. @ empty set",
 "A set is a subset of B if every element of A is in B, written A is contained in B. The empty set is a subset of every set. @ subset",
 "Two sets are equal if they have exactly the same elements; equivalent sets have the same number of elements. @ equal and equivalent",
 "The power set of A is the set of all subsets of A; if n(A) = m it has 2^m elements. @ power set"],
("List all subsets of A = {a, b} and give the number of elements of its power set.",[("Subsets with no element: { }. With one element: {a}, {b}. With two: {a, b}.","Build them by size."),("Count them: 1 + 2 + 1 = 4 subsets.","Add the sizes."),("This agrees with 2^2 = 4.","A set with m elements has 2^m subsets.")]),
("Explain roster form, set-builder form and what a subset is.",["Roster lists elements","Set-builder gives a rule","Subset: every element also in the other set"],["Thinking order or repetition matters in a set"]),
["A set is a well-defined collection of distinct objects.","Roster: list; set-builder: rule.","n(A) counts the elements.","A set with m elements has 2^m subsets."],
["n|How many subsets does {1, 2, 3} have?|8|2^3 = 8.",
 "t|The empty set is a subset of every set.|True|It has no element that could fail to be in the other set.",
 "m|{x : x is a natural number less than 4} is|{1, 2, 3}|{0, 1, 2, 3}~0 is not a natural number here;{1, 2, 3, 4}~4 is not less than 4|Roster form.",
 "n|n({a, e, i, o, u}) =|5|Five elements.",
 "t|{1, 2, 2, 3} has four elements.|False|Repeated elements count once, so there are 3.",
 "n|If n(A) = 4 the number of elements in its power set is|16|2^4.",
 "t|{1, 2, 3} and {3, 2, 1} are equal sets.|True|Order does not matter.",
 "s|A set with no elements is the ____ set.|empty/null|Definition."],
svg=(venn('A only','B only','A and B'),"Venn diagram of two overlapping sets A and B","Two sets in a Venn diagram"))
lesson(C,S,CH,'set-operations-and-de-morgan',"Union, intersection, difference, complement and De Morgan's laws","Two clubs","In a class, some pupils are in the music club and some in the sports club, and some are in both. Union, intersection and difference let us talk about each group exactly.",
["The union A U B contains elements that are in A or in B or in both. @ union",
 "The intersection A n B contains elements common to both A and B. @ intersection",
 "The difference A - B contains elements of A that are not in B. @ A - B",
 "The complement A' is the set of elements of the universal set U that are not in A. @ complement",
 "Disjoint sets have no common element, so A n B is empty. @ disjoint sets",
 "De Morgan's laws: (A U B)' = A' n B' and (A n B)' = A' U B'. @ De Morgan",
 "For finite sets, n(A U B) = n(A) + n(B) - n(A n B). @ n(A U B)"],
("In a class of 40, 25 like tea, 20 like coffee and 8 like both. How many like at least one, and how many like neither?",[("n(A U B) = 25 + 20 - 8.","Use the inclusion formula."),("= 37 like at least one.","Add and subtract."),("Neither = 40 - 37 = 3.","Complement within the class.")]),
("State De Morgan's laws in words and the formula for n(A U B).",["Complement of union is intersection of complements","Complement of intersection is union of complements","n(A U B) = n(A) + n(B) - n(A n B)"],["Forgetting to subtract the common part"]),
["A U B: in either; A n B: in both; A - B: in A not B.","A' = U - A.","De Morgan: (A U B)' = A' n B'.","n(A U B) = n(A) + n(B) - n(A n B)."],
["n|If A = {1,2,3}, B = {3,4}, how many elements does A U B have?|4|{1,2,3,4}.",
 "n|n(A n B) for A = {1,2,3}, B = {2,3,4} is|2|The common elements are 2 and 3.",
 "t|A - B and B - A are always equal.|False|For example {1,2} - {2} = {1} but {2} - {1,2} is empty.",
 "n|n(A) = 10, n(B) = 12, n(A n B) = 5. n(A U B) =|17|10 + 12 - 5.",
 "m|(A U B)' equals|A' n B'|A' U B'~That is (A n B)';A n B~Not correct|De Morgan's first law.",
 "t|Disjoint sets have an empty intersection.|True|By definition.",
 "n|In a class of 30, 18 play cricket, 15 play football, 5 play both. How many play neither?|2|18 + 15 - 5 = 28, and 30 - 28 = 2.",
 "s|The set of elements in A but not in B is the ____ A - B.|difference|Definition."],
svg=(venn('A - B','B - A','A n B','A','B'),"Venn diagram labelled with A only, both and B only","Intersection and differences"))
CH='real-numbers'
lesson(C,S,CH,'rational-and-irrational-numbers',"Rational and irrational numbers on the number line","The diagonal that is not a fraction","Draw a square of side 1 and measure its diagonal. By Pythagoras it is the square root of 2, a number that cannot be written as a fraction. Ancient mathematicians found this troubling!",
["A rational number can be written as p/q with p, q integers and q not zero; its decimal form terminates or repeats. @ rational p/q",
 "An irrational number cannot be written as p/q; its decimal form neither terminates nor repeats, like root 2 = 1.41421356... @ irrational",
 "Real numbers are the rational and irrational numbers together, and each corresponds to a point on the number line. @ real numbers",
 "Between any two real numbers there are infinitely many rational and irrational numbers. @ infinite between",
 "To find a rational number between a and b, take their average (a + b)/2. @ average",
 "To place root 2 on the number line, draw a right triangle with legs 1 and 1 at 0 and 1; the hypotenuse has length root 2 and an arc with centre 0 marks it. @ root 2 construction",
 "A repeating decimal such as 0.333... is rational: 0.333... = 1/3. @ repeating decimals"],
("Write 0.727272... as a fraction.",[("Let x = 0.7272... Multiply by 100: 100x = 72.7272...","The block 72 has two digits."),("Subtract: 100x - x = 72, so 99x = 72.","The repeating part cancels."),("x = 72/99 = 8/11.","Divide numerator and denominator by 9.")]),
("Explain how to tell a rational number from an irrational one by its decimal form.",["Rational: terminating or repeating","Irrational: non-terminating non-repeating","Both are real numbers"],["Thinking pi = 22/7 exactly"]),
["Rational = p/q; irrational cannot be written that way.","Rational decimals terminate or repeat.","Real numbers fill the number line.","Root 2 is irrational."],
["m|Which is irrational?|root 2|0.25~A terminating decimal;1/3~A fraction|Its decimal never ends or repeats.",
 "t|0.333... is a rational number.|True|It equals 1/3.",
 "t|Every real number is rational.|False|Root 2 is real but irrational.",
 "n|Find the average of 1 and 2 (a rational number between them).|1.5|(1 + 2)/2.",
 "m|0.75 as a fraction is|3/4|7/5~Mixed digits;75/10~Not simplified|75/100 = 3/4.",
 "t|pi is irrational.|True|Its decimal expansion never repeats.",
 "n|The decimal 1/4 equals|0.25|1 divided by 4.",
 "s|Numbers that cannot be written as p/q are called ____ numbers.|irrational|Definition."],
svg=(numline(0,3,[(1,'1'),(1.4142,'root 2'),(2,'2')],'root 2 is about 1.4142 - between 1 and 2'),"Number line with 1, root 2 and 2 marked","Where root 2 lies"))
lesson(C,S,CH,'surds-and-scientific-notation',"Surds, rationalising and scientific notation","Big and small numbers","The distance to the Sun is about 150 000 000 km and a virus may be 0.0000001 m wide. Scientists write such numbers compactly. And roots like root 2 need careful handling too.",
["A surd is an irrational root such as root 2, root 3 or the cube root of 5. @ surd",
 "Laws: root a x root b = root (ab) and root a / root b = root (a/b). @ surd laws",
 "Like surds can be added: 3 root 2 + 5 root 2 = 8 root 2. @ like surds",
 "Simplify surds by taking out square factors: root 18 = root 9 x root 2 = 3 root 2. @ simplify",
 "To rationalise 1/(root a) multiply the top and bottom by root a, giving root a / a. @ rationalise",
 "For 1/(a + root b) multiply by the conjugate (a - root b) to use (a + root b)(a - root b) = a squared - b. @ conjugate",
 "Scientific notation writes a number as a x 10^n with 1 less than or equal to a less than 10 and n an integer. @ a x 10^n"],
("Rationalise 1/(root 3 + 1) and write 4 500 000 in scientific notation.",[("Multiply top and bottom by (root 3 - 1): denominator = 3 - 1 = 2.","Conjugate removes the root."),("So 1/(root 3 + 1) = (root 3 - 1)/2.","Numerator is root 3 - 1."),("4 500 000 = 4.5 x 10^6.","Move the decimal point 6 places.")]),
("Describe how to rationalise a denominator and write a number in scientific notation.",["Multiply by the conjugate","Result has a rational denominator","a x 10^n with a between 1 and 10"],["Leaving a root in the denominator"]),
["Surds: root a x root b = root (ab).","Rationalise by multiplying by the conjugate.","Like surds add by adding coefficients.","Scientific notation: a x 10^n, 1 <= a < 10."],
["m|root 18 simplifies to|3 root 2|2 root 3~Wrong factor;9 root 2~Take the root of 9;6~Not correct|root 9 x root 2.",
 "n|(3 root 2) + (5 root 2) has coefficient|8|3 + 5.",
 "t|1/root 2 rationalised is root 2 / 2.|True|Multiply by root 2 on top and bottom.",
 "m|0.00045 in scientific notation is|4.5 x 10^-4|45 x 10^-5~a must be between 1 and 10;4.5 x 10^4~Wrong sign of exponent|Move 4 places right.",
 "n|(root 3 + 1)(root 3 - 1) =|2|3 - 1.",
 "t|root 2 + root 3 = root 5.|False|Unlike surds do not combine.",
 "n|3 x 10^2 equals|300|3 x 100.",
 "s|A root that is irrational, like root 5, is called a ____.|surd|Definition."])
